Yes — a few other ways exist. Worth knowing them so you see why binary search is the better choice, not just that it works.

Alternative 1: Linear scan — O(n)
python
def find_peak_linear(arr):
    for i in range(len(arr)):
        left = arr[i-1] if i > 0 else float('-inf')
        right = arr[i+1] if i < len(arr)-1 else float('-inf')
        if arr[i] > left and arr[i] > right:
            return i

This absolutely works — walk through every element, check its neighbors. It's simpler to write and reason about. The only downside is speed: O(n) instead of O(log n). For small arrays, this difference doesn't matter at all. It only matters when n is huge (millions of elements) — that's when halving the search space each step actually pays off.

Alternative 2: Sorting-based tricks

Not really applicable here — sorting would destroy the neighbor relationships a peak depends on, so this doesn't help.

Alternative 3: Divide and conquer without the "uphill/downhill" shortcut

You could also solve this with plain recursive divide-and-conquer: check the middle element against both neighbors, and if it's not a peak, recurse into whichever side has a larger neighbor. This is basically the same as the binary search version — just framed as recursion instead of a loop. Same complexity, same core idea.

So why prefer binary search here?

Because the problem explicitly asks for O(log n) — the linear scan is "correct" but doesn't meet that requirement. This is a good habit: always check if the problem is asking for a specific time complexity before reaching for binary search. If it hadn't specified O(log n), the linear scan would be a perfectly reasonable first answer — sometimes simpler and more obviously correct beats clever-but-harder-to-verify, especially under interview time pressure.

A good rule of thumb going forward: default to the simplest correct solution first (often linear), and only reach for binary search when either (a) the problem demands log n, or (b) n is large enough that linear would actually be too slow in practice.

