Example 1: piles = [3, 6, 7, 11], h = 8, answer = 4

Say Koko eats at speed k = 4 bananas/hour. For each pile, figure out how many hours it takes her to finish that pile — she needs ceil(pile_size / k) hours per pile (if there's any bananas left over, she still uses a full hour to finish them, even a partial hour still costs a whole hour).

Pile 3: ceil(3/4) = 1 hour
Pile 6: ceil(6/4) = 2 hours
Pile 7: ceil(7/4) = 2 hours
Pile 11: ceil(11/4) = 3 hours

Total: 1 + 2 + 2 + 3 = 8 hours — exactly matches h = 8. So speed 4 works.

Now check: would a slower speed, say k = 3, also work?

ceil(3/3)=1, ceil(6/3)=2, ceil(7/3)=3, ceil(11/3)=4 → total 1+2+3+4 = 10 hours — that's more than 8, so k=3 is too slow, doesn't finish in time.

So 4 is the minimum speed that still finishes within 8 hours — that's why it's the answer.

Example 2: piles = [30, 11, 23, 4, 20], h = 5, answer = 30

Here there are 5 piles and only 5 hours — the bare minimum, one pile per hour, no room to split any pile across multiple hours. Even at the fastest reasonable check, if she picks any speed less than the largest pile (30), that pile alone would take more than 1 hour, blowing the budget since there's no slack. So she's forced to eat fast enough to clear even the biggest pile in a single hour — hence k = 30, which happens to equal max(piles).

